1
00:00:08,545 --> 00:00:14,347
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only about... you say something about contentment with the basic requisites.

2
00:00:14,367 --> 00:00:26,910
[SPEAKER_01]: There's something that most people would like to have, if they thought about it, to be satisfied with that which is available, readily available to you, actually.

3
00:00:28,637 --> 00:00:35,179
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, good enough food, good enough clothing, good enough shelter and enough medicine.

4
00:00:35,199 --> 00:00:40,881
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's... even though we have those things available... People want more.

5
00:00:40,941 --> 00:00:42,481
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no contentment, generally.

6
00:00:43,461 --> 00:00:51,744
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no contentment because there is no... because people are careless in regard to their desires.

7
00:00:53,664 --> 00:00:56,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Contentment requires maintenance.

8
00:00:58,259 --> 00:01:13,284
[SPEAKER_00]: It requires you to... to foresee things before they arise, to foresee a possibility for proliferation before it arises.

9
00:01:14,585 --> 00:01:21,967
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just... obviously, there's everything else that you... if somebody's just starting, it's going to be unpleasant because of the discomfort and it's going to...

10
00:01:24,273 --> 00:01:39,966
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to require some doing and learning, but once a person develops that habit, then, yeah, they don't need to be constantly obsessing over simplicity and keeping things simple and requisites, because that would become the way they prefer to live.

11
00:01:41,247 --> 00:01:45,991
[SPEAKER_00]: But until they reach that point, you do need to keep yourself in check.

12
00:01:46,612 --> 00:01:47,572
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much it.

13
00:01:48,894 --> 00:01:52,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Just ask yourself, well, hold on, do I need this much or do I need this many?

14
00:01:53,815 --> 00:01:56,036
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can I... is this sufficient?

15
00:01:56,997 --> 00:01:59,398
[SPEAKER_00]: And most often the answer is yes, absolutely.

16
00:02:00,158 --> 00:02:09,263
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason why people always go for more is because of the unquestioned attitude towards avoiding discomfort.

17
00:02:10,083 --> 00:02:11,163
[SPEAKER_00]: People don't want discomfort.

18
00:02:11,183 --> 00:02:14,885
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, do I need a mattress of that thickness?

19
00:02:15,045 --> 00:02:15,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, not really.

20
00:02:17,006 --> 00:02:19,307
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, if I don't... if I don't have it,

21
00:02:21,861 --> 00:02:24,343
[SPEAKER_00]: sufficiently comfortable, I won't sleep at night.

22
00:02:24,623 --> 00:02:30,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you might not sleep first night or second night, where third night you'll be so tired, you'll sleep on a concrete block.

23
00:02:31,549 --> 00:02:33,870
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you train yourself in that regard.

24
00:02:34,451 --> 00:02:45,680
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, you don't have to... you don't have to do it, sort of, without a purpose, as in just... just try to renounce everything and... and... and not eat and sleep on a stone.

25
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:51,364
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you know, if that brings the unwholesome states to decrease and wholesome states to increase, yeah, by all means.

26
00:02:52,041 --> 00:02:58,926
[SPEAKER_00]: By doing it in itself... because sometimes people can get intoxicated with that sort of the dhānga, determination, which is misguided.

27
00:02:59,706 --> 00:03:04,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Like you're just doing it out of some sort of macho, you know, thing to prove.

28
00:03:04,629 --> 00:03:07,671
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, yeah, you know, I don't lay down when I sleep and so on.

29
00:03:08,092 --> 00:03:08,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

30
00:03:09,853 --> 00:03:15,897
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the relationship between that and unwholesome states decreasing and wholesome states increasing?

31
00:03:15,977 --> 00:03:17,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Usually ends up increasing the conceit and so on.

32
00:03:18,999 --> 00:03:20,400
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you always want to keep it practical.

33
00:03:21,623 --> 00:03:30,706
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you know you won't be able to kind of veer off too far into into conceited determination of I'm the best, I'm the toughest.

34
00:03:30,786 --> 00:03:42,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep it practical, like things that are genuinely practical, things that are not necessary, that can then support your practice towards wakefulness and towards diminishing of the unwholesome states.

35
00:03:45,091 --> 00:03:46,252
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, the little things.

36
00:03:46,412 --> 00:03:50,413
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to throw everything away, but yeah, ask yourself, is my mattress too soft?

37
00:03:51,407 --> 00:03:54,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Could I sleep on a harder surface?

38
00:03:54,148 --> 00:03:55,288
[SPEAKER_00]: What would the result be?

39
00:03:55,308 --> 00:04:00,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it would be less sleep, because you wouldn't be able to overly enjoy it, because it's not too comfortable.

40
00:04:01,630 --> 00:04:06,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, okay, so that could bring the unwholesome states to decrease and wholesome states to increase.

41
00:04:06,892 --> 00:04:08,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Means I won't be indulging in the sleep.

42
00:04:09,172 --> 00:04:11,373
[SPEAKER_00]: So you remove your mattress and sleep on a wooden board.

43
00:04:13,814 --> 00:04:17,255
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're doing it out of some sort of self-advertisement, well...

44
00:04:18,849 --> 00:04:21,930
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it can still arise, it can still be made wholesome.

45
00:04:24,031 --> 00:04:25,832
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in a way, it's still okay.

46
00:04:27,452 --> 00:04:29,613
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's important to distinguish the two.

47
00:04:30,493 --> 00:04:38,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's done out of just some sort of inspirational determination, or if it's done for the purpose of practically supporting your practice.

48
00:04:38,897 --> 00:04:42,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, becoming stronger, being able to... Yeah, tougher, you can take more then.

49
00:04:42,638 --> 00:04:45,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you'll be bothered less, because you can take more.

50
00:04:45,986 --> 00:04:47,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you can take physical pain.

51
00:04:47,828 --> 00:04:48,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Take more by taking this.

52
00:04:49,229 --> 00:04:50,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

53
00:04:54,153 --> 00:05:06,924
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, if your body is dependent on a certain amount of comfort, well, you would struggle if you were to leave the softness of your home, your environment, your bedroom.

54
00:05:07,938 --> 00:05:08,678
[SPEAKER_00]: You would struggle.

55
00:05:09,058 --> 00:05:22,742
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ask yourself, like, is my current situation really making me tougher and able to endure the elements, as the Buddha said that you should, because, you know, some of the things are to be abandoned by enduring.

56
00:05:24,463 --> 00:05:32,205
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, you can ask yourself honestly then, well, would I be abandoning any of those things if I can't even give up my five-inch mattress?

57
00:05:35,687 --> 00:05:41,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, you're supposed to be enduring cold and heat and mosquitoes and insects, discomfort.

58
00:05:44,834 --> 00:05:49,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Or just an evening meal, eating less as people... Sure, sure, eating less, exactly, yeah.

59
00:05:51,379 --> 00:05:51,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it necessary?

60
00:05:51,999 --> 00:05:54,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I die if I don't eat until tomorrow?

61
00:05:54,621 --> 00:05:55,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Will this hunger kill me?

62
00:05:55,842 --> 00:06:00,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I genuinely, like, fainting and, I don't know, losing my senses?

63
00:06:00,986 --> 00:06:01,146
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

64
00:06:02,347 --> 00:06:02,927
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

65
00:06:02,947 --> 00:06:04,188
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just a bit of discomfort.

66
00:06:05,682 --> 00:06:19,656
[SPEAKER_01]: There's also, in terms of shelter, trying to get your surroundings, in terms of your shelter, perfectly spiritual, in a way, so that you can practice correctly.

67
00:06:19,676 --> 00:06:20,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, sure, sure.

68
00:06:20,517 --> 00:06:21,878
[SPEAKER_01]: So that you become a stronger person.

69
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:28,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, that's, that's what, that's all rooted in that, as I said, misguided inspiration and determination.

70
00:06:31,667 --> 00:06:36,031
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like... it's more on a level of people's identity and what they do and what they're practicing.

71
00:06:36,731 --> 00:06:49,040
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not really rooted properly on the level of actually trying to uproot the identity, belongings, appropriation, sense of self, life and existence.

72
00:06:50,281 --> 00:06:53,504
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, people have a little shrine and they have a little setup.

73
00:06:54,164 --> 00:06:56,166
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, again, we have to differentiate.

74
00:06:56,186 --> 00:06:57,747
[SPEAKER_00]: We're talking about a casual

75
00:06:59,205 --> 00:07:06,007
[SPEAKER_00]: A lay person who's practicing, or you're talking about somebody who's really sort of going for it, committed to it full-on.

76
00:07:07,368 --> 00:07:09,628
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're just doing it part-time, that's fine.

77
00:07:10,629 --> 00:07:16,590
[SPEAKER_00]: These little reminders can be helpful, because, well, yeah, you're not, you're not giving up the life.

78
00:07:17,971 --> 00:07:22,132
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're actually a monk or something like that, then, yeah, it's not really...

79
00:07:23,433 --> 00:07:28,094
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, a monk who's practicing towards arahantship, or towards understanding the dhamma, at least,

80
00:07:36,661 --> 00:07:41,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Because shrines can be focuses of your intention to practice.

81
00:07:42,325 --> 00:07:44,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, not really.

82
00:07:44,807 --> 00:07:52,553
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, again, as I said, if you live a busy lay life and you have no intention of leaving it, then, yeah, a little shrine can remind you of certain values.

83
00:07:53,254 --> 00:08:03,322
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, the Buddha said, yeah, like, you know, if you're not doing anything else, go and visit the place with the target that was born and so on, because it will make you think about him, as in think about the values of enlightenment.

84
00:08:04,663 --> 00:08:04,803
[SPEAKER_00]: But

85
00:08:05,750 --> 00:08:11,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Having a shrine, a worshipping shrine, for the sake of your practice, well, it's just completely redundant.

86
00:08:11,794 --> 00:08:13,235
[SPEAKER_00]: It makes no difference, literally.

87
00:08:14,335 --> 00:08:19,519
[SPEAKER_00]: It's neither contributing... Well, it could be taken away, if you think that's what practice is.

88
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:22,921
[SPEAKER_00]: But I think most people don't think that.

89
00:08:24,762 --> 00:08:27,404
[SPEAKER_00]: They might do it out of a habit or some sort of, like, pleasing identity.

90
00:08:28,785 --> 00:08:32,007
[SPEAKER_00]: My shrine, my ajahn, my this, my that.

91
00:08:33,247 --> 00:08:34,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is fine.

92
00:08:35,636 --> 00:08:43,827
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you want to arrive at any relevant distinction, that needs to be overcome.

93
00:08:44,868 --> 00:08:53,320
[SPEAKER_01]: In terms of benefits of community life for a monk, as opposed to living alone,

94
00:08:59,666 --> 00:09:09,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think it's good for someone, a monk, to start off in a community, or just go straight into seclusion, you know, take on the suttas, read the suttas, practice?

95
00:09:09,953 --> 00:09:13,635
[SPEAKER_00]: It depends on the community, and also depends on the monk.

96
00:09:15,496 --> 00:09:23,021
[SPEAKER_00]: As in how much self-transparency has that person developed beforehand, then they might be able to make it.

97
00:09:24,042 --> 00:09:28,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's this Dhammapada verse, it says, number 61,

98
00:09:32,218 --> 00:09:39,663
[SPEAKER_01]: If, in your course, you don't meet your equal, your better, then continue your course firmly alone.

99
00:09:40,343 --> 00:09:41,784
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no fellowship with fools.

100
00:09:42,444 --> 00:09:48,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I mean, that refers to somebody who's not sure what's in a sotāpanna, because he already knows what equal to sotāpanna is.

101
00:09:48,568 --> 00:09:50,229
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, another person with the right view?

102
00:09:50,429 --> 00:09:50,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

103
00:09:51,470 --> 00:09:52,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you will be living harmoniously.

104
00:09:52,991 --> 00:09:56,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you're just pairing yourself with fools.

105
00:09:56,873 --> 00:09:59,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless they're there to learn from you, which is a different type of relationship.

106
00:10:00,136 --> 00:10:00,596
[SPEAKER_00]: The...

107
00:10:01,608 --> 00:10:11,093
[SPEAKER_00]: The person who is starting cannot apply that verse, because they don't really know what the criteria is for the right view and for the equal or better.

108
00:10:12,294 --> 00:10:12,854
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's what I mean.

109
00:10:12,874 --> 00:10:30,283
[SPEAKER_00]: If they're self-transparent, as in they're really looking internally towards things that one should be looking at, then theoretically, yeah, they could probably go straight in a more secluded environment, but highly unlikely today.

110
00:10:31,250 --> 00:10:35,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there are other things that you can learn.

111
00:10:35,491 --> 00:10:45,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Necessary things, you know, the the the rules and interaction, because today Buddhism is pretty much inseparable from its respective tradition.

112
00:10:46,173 --> 00:10:55,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, ideally, Buddhism is not a tradition and it didn't used to be, but you can't expect today to be just some pure pure setup.

113
00:10:56,314 --> 00:10:59,035
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always going to have some traditional influence, which means you always need to

114
00:10:59,557 --> 00:11:03,678
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, at least to a degree, how to relate to it, as in learn, basically.

115
00:11:04,519 --> 00:11:07,340
[SPEAKER_00]: But that brings to the next point of, well, it depends on the community.

116
00:11:08,100 --> 00:11:13,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Because some communities might be a bit too obsessed about a random tradition that has nothing to do with the practice of dhamma.

117
00:11:14,362 --> 00:11:15,362
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's all about that.

118
00:11:16,803 --> 00:11:21,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Or it's all about full-on control, telling people what to do and...

119
00:11:21,084 --> 00:11:21,324
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

120
00:11:22,445 --> 00:11:24,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Which you don't necessarily sign up for.

121
00:11:26,266 --> 00:11:27,687
[SPEAKER_00]: But I think there are...

122
00:11:27,747 --> 00:11:28,747
[SPEAKER_00]: If a person is clear with

123
00:11:29,134 --> 00:11:32,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Internally, with themselves, what they want.

124
00:11:33,616 --> 00:11:38,157
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, take it a step back, as in, don't overact out of that inspiration.

125
00:11:39,378 --> 00:11:52,483
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'm sure a person can find a suitable enough place, where you could strike the balance between, well, learning certain, yeah, ultimately unnecessary things, but practically necessary things.

126
00:11:52,563 --> 00:11:57,485
[SPEAKER_01]: And having time to practice with yourself correctly, with the suttas and everything else.

127
00:12:03,232 --> 00:12:06,254
[SPEAKER_01]: There's another verse, sutta, I'll read out.

128
00:12:07,215 --> 00:12:09,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Anguttara, 8, verse 30.

129
00:12:11,118 --> 00:12:16,362
[SPEAKER_01]: It says that dhamma is for one who is reclusive, not for one who is entangled.

130
00:12:17,723 --> 00:12:20,985
[SPEAKER_01]: And thus was it said, with reference to what was it said.

131
00:12:21,285 --> 00:12:28,050
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a case where a monk, when living in seclusion, is visited by monks, nuns, laymen, laborers,

132
00:12:29,592 --> 00:12:45,886
[SPEAKER_01]: And with his mind bent on seclusion, tending towards seclusion, inclined towards seclusion, aiming at seclusion, enjoying renunciation, he converses with them only as much as is necessary for them to take their leave.

133
00:12:46,867 --> 00:12:51,571
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who is reclusive, not for one in entanglement.

134
00:12:55,143 --> 00:12:56,023
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's the thing.

135
00:12:56,083 --> 00:13:04,726
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to, as I said, you have to find that balance or find a place that has a balance.

136
00:13:04,806 --> 00:13:15,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there are monasteries in Thailand, for example, where you meet in the morning for pindapat and chores and the rest of the day you're on your own in your kuti.

137
00:13:15,570 --> 00:13:17,550
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're practicing seclusion but within the community.

138
00:13:17,571 --> 00:13:25,033
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if something comes up, occasionally they would be, you know, getting together to study, junior monks, whatever, vinaya, saṅgha,

139
00:13:25,782 --> 00:13:26,482
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all fine.

140
00:13:26,562 --> 00:13:32,905
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, on the other hand, you have those monasteries who sort of have all these goodies, but then you constantly have to be mingling with each other.

141
00:13:34,065 --> 00:13:45,889
[SPEAKER_00]: So, despite having the potential for that kind of reclusive life, and minimum of coming together and socializing, you end up spending most of your days socializing.

142
00:13:46,789 --> 00:13:54,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, at five o'clock we have puja, at six o'clock we have pīndapāda, at eight o'clock we have chores, at ten o'clock we have meals,

143
00:13:54,478 --> 00:13:56,300
[SPEAKER_00]: At one o'clock, we have clean-up after the meal.

144
00:13:56,380 --> 00:14:00,864
[SPEAKER_00]: At three o'clock, we have, I don't know, dying robe session.

145
00:14:01,464 --> 00:14:02,805
[SPEAKER_00]: At five o'clock, we have tea.

146
00:14:02,905 --> 00:14:04,226
[SPEAKER_00]: At six o'clock, we have puja.

147
00:14:04,527 --> 00:14:06,368
[SPEAKER_00]: At nine o'clock, you go to your kuti alone.

148
00:14:07,589 --> 00:14:09,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's not bent on seclusion.

149
00:14:09,071 --> 00:14:10,812
[SPEAKER_00]: It's constantly living with a group.

150
00:14:11,653 --> 00:14:17,438
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is... there is a genuine factor of being physically secluded.

151
00:14:20,072 --> 00:14:33,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, yeah, in comparison to a busy lay life, sure, a group of people in the monastery are quieter and more secluded, but fundamentally, from the point of view of practicing the dhamma correctly, no, physical seclusion is necessary.

152
00:14:34,295 --> 00:14:41,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you're not talking to each other, just sitting next to each other throughout the day, meditating together, well, you're still not physically secluded.

153
00:14:42,397 --> 00:14:43,857
[SPEAKER_00]: And to that extent, you're a loss.

154
00:14:45,718 --> 00:14:46,418
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

155
00:14:46,458 --> 00:14:58,320
[SPEAKER_00]: To find that balance whereby there will be some interaction, certainly for the first few nisaya years, but... but then that doesn't mean, like, you subscribe to 80% of your day interaction.

156
00:14:59,360 --> 00:15:03,641
[SPEAKER_00]: That quickly gets out of hand, pretty much.

157
00:15:05,121 --> 00:15:08,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, ultimately, you're training to be able to live entirely alone.

158
00:15:09,962 --> 00:15:15,383
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only way to train that is to start being alone, bit by bit by bit.

159
00:15:16,347 --> 00:15:20,888
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you spend your time just constantly being with others, well, you're certainly not training in solitude.

160
00:15:22,348 --> 00:15:33,431
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why many, many people from the, from the busy communities would be quite, well, they wouldn't be at ease if they were just dropped in a random forest, cutting on their own.

161
00:15:34,831 --> 00:15:37,471
[SPEAKER_00]: It would take time to get used to it, if they get used to it.

162
00:15:39,052 --> 00:15:40,252
[SPEAKER_01]: And I saw that in Thailand as well.

163
00:15:41,409 --> 00:15:47,052
[SPEAKER_00]: Some people were genuinely scared of the idea, or even going into the environment that nobody can speak English, for example.

164
00:15:47,272 --> 00:15:48,253
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just like...

165
00:15:49,473 --> 00:15:53,335
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, you can't expect to be able to do that overnight.

166
00:15:53,616 --> 00:15:56,597
[SPEAKER_00]: But unless you're trying, you're certainly not going to do it over hundreds of nights.

167
00:15:57,218 --> 00:15:57,738
[SPEAKER_00]: You just won't.

168
00:15:57,778 --> 00:16:00,719
[SPEAKER_00]: It won't happen overnight, nor magically by itself.

169
00:16:02,560 --> 00:16:04,662
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that needs to be your goal.

170
00:16:05,462 --> 00:16:10,985
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you recognize that weakness in yourself, that you're afraid to be alone, or you might not be able to handle it,

171
00:16:11,261 --> 00:16:21,145
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to start training and exposing yourself to it, bit by bit by bit, which means that the monastery you're in has to at least support it, has to at least allow possibility for it.

172
00:16:25,567 --> 00:16:28,888
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the benefit, other than learning the rules?

173
00:16:30,008 --> 00:16:33,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the benefit could also be that gradual exposure to solitude.

174
00:16:34,330 --> 00:16:36,791
[SPEAKER_00]: But your aim is solitude, from the start.

175
00:16:38,122 --> 00:16:48,445
[SPEAKER_01]: What if your aim is to be more conducive to others?

176
00:16:48,465 --> 00:16:54,067
[SPEAKER_01]: That you can go in the world, wherever you go, wherever there's a human being, you can be conducive.

177
00:16:54,307 --> 00:16:54,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, yeah.

178
00:16:54,987 --> 00:17:00,829
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you should be aiming for solitude, because solitude is necessary for Arahantship, and that's the ultimate conduciveness for others.

179
00:17:01,490 --> 00:17:02,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Freedom from greed, aversion, delusion.

180
00:17:04,212 --> 00:17:07,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Because community life can challenge you, you know, with other people.

181
00:17:07,735 --> 00:17:08,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, lay life can challenge you.

182
00:17:12,860 --> 00:17:15,242
[SPEAKER_01]: There's another sutta.

183
00:17:17,464 --> 00:17:19,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Samyutta Nikaya, 21, verse 10.

184
00:17:22,348 --> 00:17:26,032
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's this... an elder monk who lives alone.

185
00:17:28,619 --> 00:17:35,322
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Blessed One says to him, Is it true, Elder, that you live alone and extol the virtues of living alone?

186
00:17:35,762 --> 00:17:36,502
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, Yes, Lord.

187
00:17:37,323 --> 00:17:40,684
[SPEAKER_01]: But how do you live alone and extol the virtues of living alone?

188
00:17:42,505 --> 00:17:51,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Lord, alone I enter the village for alms, alone I return, alone I sit withdrawn in meditation, alone I do walking meditation.

189
00:17:52,309 --> 00:17:55,790
[SPEAKER_01]: That is how I live alone and extol the virtues of living alone.

190
00:17:58,117 --> 00:18:00,560
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha says, there is that way of living alone.

191
00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:03,763
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't say that there isn't.

192
00:18:04,904 --> 00:18:11,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Still, listen well to how your living alone is perfected in its details and pay close attention.

193
00:18:11,531 --> 00:18:12,132
[SPEAKER_01]: I will speak.

194
00:18:13,807 --> 00:18:14,728
[SPEAKER_01]: So this one said.

195
00:18:15,868 --> 00:18:19,030
[SPEAKER_01]: And how is living alone perfected in its details?

196
00:18:19,070 --> 00:18:34,519
[SPEAKER_01]: There is the case where whatever is past is abandoned, whatever is future is relinquished, and any passion and desire with regard to states of being attained in the present is well subdued.

197
00:18:35,380 --> 00:18:39,082
[SPEAKER_01]: That is how living alone is perfected in its details.

198
00:18:41,377 --> 00:18:53,187
[SPEAKER_01]: All-conquering, all-knowing, intelligent, with regard to all things, unadhering, all-abandoning, released in the ending of craving, him I call a man who lives alone.

199
00:18:55,188 --> 00:18:56,289
[SPEAKER_00]: By the way, what's the battery like?

200
00:19:00,472 --> 00:19:00,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it filming?

201
00:19:01,033 --> 00:19:01,593
[SPEAKER_00]: How many minutes?

202
00:19:02,414 --> 00:19:02,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Ninety.

203
00:19:02,994 --> 00:19:05,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, ten.

204
00:19:06,057 --> 00:19:06,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

205
00:19:06,957 --> 00:19:07,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Ten minutes.

206
00:19:11,081 --> 00:19:11,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so...

207
00:19:11,961 --> 00:19:16,462
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're saying that's... that's how you truly live alone, is to abandon craving, basically.

208
00:19:16,482 --> 00:19:16,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

209
00:19:17,883 --> 00:19:27,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that brings me to the other point that I should have mentioned before, is that sometimes people, monks, can use solitude as a form of avoiding things.

210
00:19:28,705 --> 00:19:33,186
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you run to a kuti, you lock yourself in, and then just... because you don't want to deal with anything.

211
00:19:34,146 --> 00:19:38,127
[SPEAKER_00]: But that includes... you don't want to deal with hardship, discomfort,

212
00:19:39,718 --> 00:19:58,303
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's... that's also done for wrong reasons, which means you need to be... you need to be making sure that your motivation towards solitude is rooted towards practice, not so... not towards avoiding, not towards just not being hassled by unpleasant things.

213
00:19:58,943 --> 00:20:01,144
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just lock your door and you don't leave at all.

214
00:20:02,344 --> 00:20:05,285
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can... you can easily see whether it's rooted in that.

215
00:20:05,325 --> 00:20:09,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Just... just... sometimes somebody will come to your kutis and see if you're upset.

216
00:20:09,786 --> 00:20:11,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you upset that somebody's disturbing your solitude?

217
00:20:12,208 --> 00:20:14,610
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are, it means you're holding on to it wrongly.

218
00:20:16,151 --> 00:20:19,734
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, if you're overly joyed that somebody came to your kuti.

219
00:20:20,194 --> 00:20:24,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Nikuli means, okay, so you're struggling with solitude, so you're just looking for distraction.

220
00:20:25,138 --> 00:20:34,846
[SPEAKER_00]: But, either of those things, or rather both of those things, can be overcome on the basis of solitude, not on the basis of socializing.

221
00:20:36,067 --> 00:20:38,709
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like that other sutta, I don't know which one, but I remember

222
00:20:40,162 --> 00:20:57,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Reading it, where the Buddha said, even if a monk goes and lives in the forest, and he just spends his time sleeping, and being sluggish, and not practicing, well, eventually, his mind will sort of calm down, and get established upon that inclusion, and then the wholesome states will increase.

223
00:20:58,581 --> 00:21:01,745
[SPEAKER_00]: On the other hand, you have a monk who lives near the village.

224
00:21:02,485 --> 00:21:10,194
[SPEAKER_00]: And although he's not sluggish and unmindful, because he lives near a village, where there are people frequenting and coming, he will decline there.

225
00:21:11,576 --> 00:21:12,236
[SPEAKER_00]: He won't increase.

226
00:21:13,137 --> 00:21:13,818
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's what I mean.

227
00:21:14,239 --> 00:21:17,422
[SPEAKER_00]: The solitude needs to be the basis, but obviously it's not a goal in itself.

228
00:21:18,003 --> 00:21:19,685
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the Buddha points out in this sutta.

229
00:21:20,673 --> 00:21:37,079
[SPEAKER_00]: It's good that you're alone, yeah, that's form of solitude, but now you want to take that solitude to the point of understanding and freeing yourself from greed, aversion, delusion, from desire, which is how you're truly alone.

230
00:21:37,119 --> 00:21:44,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as you said, you know, abandoning the past... Yeah, yeah, exactly.

231
00:21:44,782 --> 00:21:46,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandoning the past, abandoning the

232
00:21:48,143 --> 00:22:01,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandoning the emotional connection with your past, when people, you know, when... that's why people are afraid to be alone, because the mind starts thinking about things that were done in the past, could have been done, or shouldn't have been done, and all that.

233
00:22:01,909 --> 00:22:15,015
[SPEAKER_00]: It just becomes a serious... it can be a serious issue, or ends up thinking what I'm going to do tomorrow, or can't wait for tomorrow, when I'm going to meet people, and then do this, and then do that, or hoping for things tomorrow to come up, to be done, so I won't be in solitude, because I find it too hard.

234
00:22:16,608 --> 00:22:23,096
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, he abandons that, and then he fully discerns what's presently there, and that's how he's truly alone.

235
00:22:28,003 --> 00:22:30,686
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how to not procrastinate?

236
00:22:31,387 --> 00:22:31,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

237
00:22:31,988 --> 00:22:32,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Procrastinate.

238
00:22:33,370 --> 00:22:34,291
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, put things off.

239
00:22:36,602 --> 00:22:40,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there's one sutta that says, I have to go on a long journey tomorrow.

240
00:22:41,043 --> 00:22:42,283
[SPEAKER_01]: I better rest now.

241
00:22:43,664 --> 00:22:45,204
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, I don't have time to practice.

242
00:22:45,284 --> 00:22:46,624
[SPEAKER_01]: I've got, you know, things to do tomorrow.

243
00:22:46,784 --> 00:22:47,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, right, right.

244
00:22:47,945 --> 00:22:49,365
[SPEAKER_01]: I just recovered from an illness.

245
00:22:49,665 --> 00:22:49,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

246
00:22:49,905 --> 00:22:51,226
[SPEAKER_01]: I've got to, you know, take it easy.

247
00:22:51,526 --> 00:22:52,226
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't practice.

248
00:22:53,426 --> 00:22:54,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, I am ill.

249
00:22:54,546 --> 00:22:55,407
[SPEAKER_01]: I better, you know...

250
00:22:55,427 --> 00:22:55,987
[SPEAKER_00]: I better not.

251
00:22:56,127 --> 00:22:56,967
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it's the opposite.

252
00:22:57,047 --> 00:22:58,007
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, I am ill.

253
00:22:59,027 --> 00:23:00,608
[SPEAKER_01]: I might not have time to practice.

254
00:23:00,628 --> 00:23:01,348
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I better practice.

255
00:23:01,368 --> 00:23:02,388
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I better practice now.

256
00:23:03,249 --> 00:23:05,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's exactly how to not procrastinate.

257
00:23:06,535 --> 00:23:08,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, to think of the alternative as well.

258
00:23:08,676 --> 00:23:09,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, wait a minute.

259
00:23:10,416 --> 00:23:11,256
[SPEAKER_00]: What's more important?

260
00:23:11,937 --> 00:23:13,317
[SPEAKER_00]: To rest and go on a trip tomorrow?

261
00:23:13,557 --> 00:23:16,738
[SPEAKER_00]: That might be unavoidable, but that's not the question.

262
00:23:16,758 --> 00:23:19,178
[SPEAKER_00]: The question is, which is more important?

263
00:23:19,198 --> 00:23:24,560
[SPEAKER_00]: The random trip that happened to be unavoidable due to circumstances, or your practice of dhamma?

264
00:23:25,720 --> 00:23:26,680
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know the answer.

265
00:23:27,941 --> 00:23:29,301
[SPEAKER_00]: It's far more important to practice dhamma.

266
00:23:29,321 --> 00:23:32,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you make sure that whenever you can practice, you practice it.

267
00:23:33,082 --> 00:23:35,963
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't use other things that are not equally important

268
00:23:36,827 --> 00:23:39,929
[SPEAKER_00]: As an excuse to not make the effort towards the dhamma.

269
00:23:41,810 --> 00:23:45,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, it simplifies your life by having a clear goal.

270
00:23:45,673 --> 00:23:46,834
[SPEAKER_00]: It does, yeah, yeah, exactly.

271
00:23:46,854 --> 00:23:47,854
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about practice, nothing else.

272
00:23:47,954 --> 00:23:48,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

273
00:23:48,515 --> 00:23:53,678
[SPEAKER_00]: So... No, that's exactly what it contributes to.

274
00:23:53,718 --> 00:23:57,320
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about robes, it's not about food, it's not about shelter, it's not about praise.

275
00:23:58,301 --> 00:23:58,461
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

276
00:23:58,601 --> 00:23:59,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why did I become a monk?

277
00:24:05,985 --> 00:24:07,766
[SPEAKER_00]: And then make sure you relate to that answer.

278
00:24:07,986 --> 00:24:08,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Why did I become a monk?

279
00:24:09,687 --> 00:24:10,427
[SPEAKER_00]: To practice dhamma.

280
00:24:10,707 --> 00:24:12,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, so is there a connection with that practice of dhamma?

281
00:24:12,728 --> 00:24:14,089
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, am I practicing dhamma?

282
00:24:15,630 --> 00:24:19,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Or has it just been sort of pushed in some sort of abstract background?

283
00:24:20,272 --> 00:24:27,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby, yes, of course, I'm here to practice dhamma, but really, practically, your choices and things you do have nothing to do with practicing the dhamma.

284
00:24:29,477 --> 00:24:31,658
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean you don't go and don't do anything around.

285
00:24:32,418 --> 00:24:34,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Things don't necessarily need to be a distraction.

286
00:24:34,774 --> 00:24:38,937
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you don't want to be doing anything on account of doing the dhamma practice.

287
00:24:39,718 --> 00:24:42,180
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you can do other things within that practice of dhamma, that's fine.

288
00:24:43,561 --> 00:24:45,403
[SPEAKER_00]: But, that's the important... that's the criteria.

289
00:24:45,443 --> 00:24:52,549
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the... that's the criteria you use to judge the other types of activity.

290
00:24:56,912 --> 00:25:00,095
[SPEAKER_00]: And it does, again, comes down to what we spoke before, being practical.

291
00:25:02,816 --> 00:25:04,818
[SPEAKER_00]: As in doing things that have practical purpose.

292
00:25:09,061 --> 00:25:10,302
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess I was just lazy.

293
00:25:10,742 --> 00:25:13,044
[SPEAKER_01]: You might have those reasons, I suppose.

294
00:25:13,685 --> 00:25:15,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, laziness is a factor.

295
00:25:15,166 --> 00:25:19,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, lack of energy, laziness, sloth and torpor.

296
00:25:20,670 --> 00:25:23,213
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's that's one of the hindrances that needs to be overcome.

297
00:25:25,414 --> 00:25:27,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're practicing the dhamma correctly, as in

298
00:25:30,842 --> 00:25:43,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Not tolerating the unwholesome states in your mind, making effort to not engage with them, and making effort for the wholesome states, for mindfulness to be established and so on, it's diligence.

299
00:25:45,167 --> 00:25:46,108
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a proper diligence.

300
00:25:46,208 --> 00:25:48,188
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like doing the work when work is necessary.

301
00:25:48,769 --> 00:25:52,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine a chore just presents itself, and you just have to go and do it.

302
00:25:52,310 --> 00:25:53,150
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't postpone it.

303
00:25:54,751 --> 00:25:57,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Like living like here, when it starts to rain, for example, and your roof just suddenly

304
00:25:59,031 --> 00:26:01,052
[SPEAKER_00]: This new, like, leak, that wasn't there before.

305
00:26:03,654 --> 00:26:07,096
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to go and fix it, despite the discomfort of heavy wind and rain.

306
00:26:08,297 --> 00:26:09,278
[SPEAKER_00]: You end up hanging off the roof.

307
00:26:10,218 --> 00:26:16,582
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have to go and fix it, because otherwise, well, you're going to wait until tomorrow, but you might not stop the rain, and then, well, you'd be flooded inside.

308
00:26:18,103 --> 00:26:25,488
[SPEAKER_00]: So, imagine how much more important then is to have that kind of attitude towards the unwholesome states, to abandon them, and towards wholesome states, to cultivate them.

309
00:26:26,858 --> 00:26:27,898
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's diligence.

310
00:26:28,018 --> 00:26:37,460
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not diligence because, you know, you walk like a like a slow-motion robot to observe every moment that passes you, committed to the point of neurosis.

311
00:26:37,580 --> 00:26:37,740
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

312
00:26:38,720 --> 00:26:41,641
[SPEAKER_00]: It's diligence because you recognize the work and you always do it.

313
00:26:42,101 --> 00:26:43,121
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't procrastinate.

314
00:26:44,321 --> 00:26:48,222
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the unwholesome... That's that sutta where the Buddha was... the fear and dread.

315
00:26:49,042 --> 00:26:55,063
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, if I would be walking and then some fear or unwholesome states arose, I wouldn't change the posture until I dispel it first.

316
00:26:55,697 --> 00:27:08,646
[SPEAKER_00]: That was done firstly because he did... if he were to act out of it, sorry, if he were to change his posture on account of that fear or unwholesome states, his action would have been rooted in that unwholesome state.

317
00:27:08,766 --> 00:27:13,930
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he wanted to prevent that and also he wanted to prevent postponing of dealing with whatever has arisen.

318
00:27:13,990 --> 00:27:15,871
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I'll move after I finish this.

319
00:27:17,212 --> 00:27:19,153
[SPEAKER_00]: It has arisen, that's the work for you.

320
00:27:19,674 --> 00:27:23,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you didn't ask for it, doesn't mean you're not responsible.

321
00:27:25,158 --> 00:27:28,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's all it takes, with unwholesome states, to ignore them.

322
00:27:29,360 --> 00:27:39,846
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you'll be acting out of them, which then brings more unwholesome states, which then you can only... That's why it's so easy to be ignorant, to give in to lust and aversion.

323
00:27:39,986 --> 00:27:41,707
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's natural, with the grain.

324
00:27:43,448 --> 00:27:45,188
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's where the diligence of practice is.

325
00:27:46,349 --> 00:27:49,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning that, not tolerating, not welcoming.

326
00:27:49,591 --> 00:27:50,791
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, not denying it either.

327
00:27:50,891 --> 00:27:51,592
[SPEAKER_00]: It has arisen.

328
00:27:51,832 --> 00:27:52,272
[SPEAKER_00]: That's fine.

329
00:27:52,632 --> 00:27:53,873
[SPEAKER_00]: But being able to withstand it.

330
00:27:55,080 --> 00:28:00,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Being able to allow it to persist while it's changing, sort of, while it's becoming different, yet still the same.

331
00:28:01,301 --> 00:28:02,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Being able to endure it, basically.

332
00:28:02,662 --> 00:28:03,342
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I'm saying.

333
00:28:04,802 --> 00:28:09,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Without acting out of it, without ignoring it, without trying to distract yourself from it.

334
00:28:11,103 --> 00:28:11,963
[SPEAKER_00]: And that requires work.

335
00:28:12,464 --> 00:28:12,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing else.

336
00:28:13,444 --> 00:28:18,765
[SPEAKER_01]: What about, like, you have a fear arise, but then you think, it's because of my past actions.

337
00:28:20,407 --> 00:28:20,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

338
00:28:21,608 --> 00:28:22,988
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're avoiding the responsibility.

339
00:28:24,189 --> 00:28:25,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it is because of your past actions.

340
00:28:28,050 --> 00:28:30,051
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how the fear arose or why.

341
00:28:30,531 --> 00:28:33,672
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is that what you're doing now in regard to it.

342
00:28:34,353 --> 00:28:35,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you acting out of it?

343
00:28:35,773 --> 00:28:36,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you running away from it?

344
00:28:36,714 --> 00:28:37,654
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you trying to get rid of it?

345
00:28:38,574 --> 00:28:42,076
[SPEAKER_00]: If yes, that's your responsibility.

346
00:28:42,136 --> 00:28:42,676
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter.

347
00:28:42,756 --> 00:28:44,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it did arise because of your past actions.

348
00:28:44,637 --> 00:28:45,297
[SPEAKER_00]: It makes no difference.

349
00:28:45,597 --> 00:28:47,578
[SPEAKER_01]: You said this was abandoning the past.

350
00:28:48,473 --> 00:28:50,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's how you abandon the past in that present.

351
00:28:58,660 --> 00:29:00,522
[SPEAKER_01]: There are unwholesome states when you deal with it.

352
00:29:01,622 --> 00:29:01,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

353
00:29:03,344 --> 00:29:04,705
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't distract yourself from it.

354
00:29:05,205 --> 00:29:09,148
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's unwholesome in terms of sensuality, well, you don't indulge it, you don't welcome it, you don't entertain it.

355
00:29:10,389 --> 00:29:14,672
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't let it linger, as in, as in with like a allowing attitude.

356
00:29:15,178 --> 00:29:18,699
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's also the cultivating of wholesome.

357
00:29:19,619 --> 00:29:21,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Automatically, sure, sure, sure, sure.

358
00:29:21,800 --> 00:29:22,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

359
00:29:24,140 --> 00:29:27,501
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, okay, now I've dispelled the unwholesome, let me find the wholesome to do.

360
00:29:27,561 --> 00:29:27,701
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

361
00:29:27,721 --> 00:29:29,921
[SPEAKER_00]: Dispelling unwholesome, it's what wholesome is.

362
00:29:32,062 --> 00:29:36,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, automatically, if you're reducing unwholesome, you're automatically increasing the wholesome.

363
00:29:38,903 --> 00:29:41,544
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're decreasing wholesome, you're increasing unwholesome.

364
00:29:44,234 --> 00:29:46,796
[SPEAKER_00]: To the same extent, they cannot coexist.

365
00:29:49,418 --> 00:29:53,261
Which means you need to... you need to have solitude.

366
00:30:08,376 --> 00:30:14,640
[SPEAKER_00]: To be able to have clarity of your thoughts, because if you're constantly distracted with communication with others, sounds of others, you won't.

367
00:30:14,860 --> 00:30:16,742
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't develop the same level of discernment.

368
00:30:17,782 --> 00:30:25,147
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you do live in solitude, but then, don't just do some sort of things to distract you from yourself.

369
00:30:25,388 --> 00:30:28,049
[SPEAKER_00]: And often people end up doing meditation for that purpose.

370
00:30:28,089 --> 00:30:33,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'll sit and meditate, because if I just sit and do nothing, it becomes a bit too much.

371
00:30:34,214 --> 00:30:34,954
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

372
00:30:35,014 --> 00:30:36,235
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you're training against.

373
00:30:37,472 --> 00:30:45,020
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to be able to think properly, without being overwhelmed by that fear of boredom or anxiety, in that solitude.

374
00:30:46,182 --> 00:30:56,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Because how can you discern presently arisen unwholesome thought or wholesome thought, if you're refusing to think, if you're just trying to shunt the thinking out?

375
00:30:57,384 --> 00:30:58,185
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you do the work?

376
00:30:58,665 --> 00:30:59,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

377
00:30:59,485 --> 00:30:59,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

378
00:31:00,026 --> 00:31:01,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

379
00:31:01,847 --> 00:31:15,996
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to start thinking and allowing those thoughts to arise, and then learning how not to act out of it, then learning how to remove the attitude of welcoming the central thoughts, remove the attitude of trying to get rid of the unpleasant thoughts.

380
00:31:18,438 --> 00:31:19,698
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the thoughts are not the problem.

381
00:31:22,020 --> 00:31:24,321
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that attitude based on how you feel in regard to them.

382
00:31:24,421 --> 00:31:25,102
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the problem.

383
00:31:26,608 --> 00:31:31,572
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're ignoring them, trying to stop thinking or whatever else, how can you see the problem?

384
00:31:31,652 --> 00:31:33,474
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't see the problem, how can you solve the problem?

385
00:31:37,718 --> 00:31:42,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're not successful to a degree, at least you're doing that work, you know.

386
00:31:42,842 --> 00:31:43,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

387
00:31:43,783 --> 00:31:45,965
[SPEAKER_00]: The success can come out of the right type of work.

388
00:31:46,450 --> 00:31:47,411
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a persistence.

389
00:31:47,731 --> 00:31:48,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

390
00:31:48,712 --> 00:31:49,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Trial and error.

391
00:31:49,212 --> 00:31:50,974
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the right type of work, in a way.

392
00:31:51,434 --> 00:31:52,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

393
00:31:52,715 --> 00:31:55,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's why you don't get more specific instruction in the suttas.

394
00:31:56,418 --> 00:32:01,242
[SPEAKER_00]: He's diligent, he arises energy, he doesn't he doesn't tolerate unwholesome, he cultivates wholesome.

395
00:32:02,042 --> 00:32:05,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that is the closest you can get, in terms of instructing somebody.

396
00:32:07,847 --> 00:32:08,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, persistence.

397
00:32:09,948 --> 00:32:12,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Have I attained that which hasn't been attained?

398
00:32:12,310 --> 00:32:12,570
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

399
00:32:13,671 --> 00:32:15,753
[SPEAKER_00]: No excuse, then, for lacking energy.

400
00:32:18,005 --> 00:32:20,166
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't use alms food for the wrong reason.

401
00:32:20,186 --> 00:32:22,227
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't use his road food for the wrong reason.

402
00:32:22,327 --> 00:32:24,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything then becomes aimed for that right purpose.

403
00:32:29,210 --> 00:32:41,016
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you pair that with how we started this whole discussion, having a degree of discomfort in your life, yeah, that will keep you, keep you, well, it will keep you mindful of that basis.

404
00:32:42,236 --> 00:32:47,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you might think, oh no, I'm striving properly, I'm cultivating, while your whole life revolves around comfort.

405
00:32:50,701 --> 00:32:59,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Access to medicines, and robes, and cushy bedrooms, and so on.

406
00:32:59,705 --> 00:33:06,349
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you ask yourself, well, actually, is there a likelihood of this being an impediment?

407
00:33:08,250 --> 00:33:10,111
[SPEAKER_00]: And, more often than not, the answer is yes.

408
00:33:11,752 --> 00:33:14,553
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you have a degree of discomfort, you can't get too complacent.

409
00:33:16,687 --> 00:33:23,410
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, even on the days when you really don't have much energy, well, you won't be too complacent, as in you won't be too lazy, because you can't.

410
00:33:24,350 --> 00:33:26,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can't sleep too much on a hard wooden plank.

411
00:33:28,532 --> 00:33:33,554
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too cold to just sort of, you know, sit and do nothing or read some poetry or whatever else.

412
00:33:33,794 --> 00:33:34,134
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

413
00:33:35,094 --> 00:33:36,715
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just keep your mind sharp.

414
00:33:38,856 --> 00:33:44,678
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why usually solitude and a degree of discomfort vary, obviously, for different people varies.

415
00:33:46,196 --> 00:33:52,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Would be bringing the unwholesome states to decrease and wholesome states to increase.

416
00:33:53,483 --> 00:33:53,683
Yeah.

417
00:33:54,384 --> 00:33:56,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Toughen you up, basically.

418
00:33:56,386 --> 00:33:57,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Do what's important.

419
00:33:57,787 --> 00:33:57,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

420
00:33:58,348 --> 00:33:59,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Do what's necessary.

421
00:33:59,369 --> 00:34:00,370
[SPEAKER_00]: What's necessary, exactly.

422
00:34:00,670 --> 00:34:00,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

423
00:34:02,331 --> 00:34:03,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you just can't afford.

424
00:34:04,013 --> 00:34:04,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality.

425
00:34:05,054 --> 00:34:06,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

